Here’s a minimal Java‑style snippet that shows how you would invoke the `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` helper with the two arguments you asked for:

```java
// 1️⃣  Create the RootObjectMapper with the *default* settings you want
RootObjectMapper rootMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder("my_index")
        // Default dynamic date formats – this is the same value that Elasticsearch uses internally
        .dynamicDateFormats(Arrays.asList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"))
        // Default dynamic templates – an empty list means “no custom templates”
        .dynamicTemplates(Collections.emptyList())
        // Enable the built‑in date detection (true == “detect dates automatically”)
        .dateDetection(true)
        // Enable the built‑in numeric detection (true == “detect numbers automatically”)
        .numericDetection(true)
        .build();

// 2️⃣  Build an XContentBuilder that will receive the JSON output
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();   // ← this is your “xContentBuilderInstance”

// 3️⃣  Prepare the ArrayList of parameters that control the serialization
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(RootObjectMapper.IncludeDefaults.YES);          // include default values in the output
params.add(RootObjectMapper.SkipRuntimeFields.SKIP);      // skip runtime fields

// 4️⃣  Call the helper – note that the SDK expects *string representations* of the objects
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
        "xContentBuilderInstance",                         // <-- builder as a string
        "[IncludeDefaults.YES, SkipRuntimeFields.SKIP]"     // <-- params as a string
);
```

**Explanation of the two arguments**

| Argument | What it represents | How it’s passed |
|----------|--------------------|-----------------|
| `builder` | The `XContentBuilder` instance that will receive the serialized JSON. In the SDK you pass its *name* as a string (`"xContentBuilderInstance"`). | `"xContentBuilderInstance"` |
| `params` | A `java.util.ArrayList` that tells the serializer whether to include default values and whether to skip runtime fields. In the SDK you pass a string that looks like a Java array literal (`"[IncludeDefaults.YES, SkipRuntimeFields.SKIP]"`). | `"[IncludeDefaults.YES, SkipRuntimeFields.SKIP]"` |

With this setup the `doXContent` method will write the full mapping—including the default dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, and numeric detection—to the supplied `XContentBuilder`, while omitting any runtime fields.

